index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..ffb11d346a2b991a51772a7f7ec16c6eb73f6244
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ttnn/cpp/ttnn/operations/kvcache_manager/docs/design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,418 @@
+# KV Cache Device Protocol and Operation Invariants
+
+This document is the implementation reference for the TTNN operations, wire ABI,
+and device kernels consumed by the external `kvcache-manager` package. Start
+with the [component README](../README.md) for the repository boundary.
+
+`Client` and `Server` below name protocol roles. Their product C++ and Python
+classes, lifecycle, host storage, and server host runtime are owned and built by
+`kvcache-manager`, not TTNN.
+
+The exact wire ABI and address calculations are defined by
+[`packet.hpp`](../common/protocol/packet.hpp) and [`layout.hpp`](../common/protocol/layout.hpp).
+Those headers, not prose or copied offset tables, are authoritative for numeric layout.
+
+“Durable” below means written through to host memory owned by the live Server
+role. The cache is volatile: device buffers, mapped memory, and host values are
+destroyed by manager Server shutdown, process exit, or device reset.
+
+## 1. Roles and ownership
+
+The device DRAM directory is authoritative for key membership, value length, access recency, and
+device-cache residency. Once a PUT commits, host storage owns the authoritative value bytes.
+
+| Worker | Owns | Does not own |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Client RISCV_0 | PUT source-DRAM reads and the Client value-ring producer | Fabric and Server state |
+| Client RISCV_1 | PUT Fabric sends, lease/grant completion, and scalar GET acknowledgement | Source reads and Server directory state |
+| Server `{0,0}` NCRISC | Server Fabric connections, request dispatch, GET output writes, Client completion | Directory, allocator, LRU, and host values |
+| Server `{0,0}` BRISC | Directory, hash index, buddy allocator, residency LRU, and GET-value CB production | Fabric connections |
+| Optional GET ingress on `{1,0}` | Mapped-host descriptor validation and Server-ring publication | Fabric, directory, and value data |
+| Optional GET admission on `{2,0}` | GET-CB mirror reads and device-cache fills | Host production, Fabric, directory, and allocator policy |
+| Host tiering thread | Host-value lifetime, pool allocation, and mapped or PCIe staging | Device directory and Fabric |
+
+Host code configures, launches, retains, and terminates these workers. A fallback replaces an entire
+stage; it never gives two workers authority over the same directory, allocator, or Fabric channel.
+
+One Server accepts up to `kMaxClients` Clients. Each Client is sequential, and the Server dispatch
+loop serializes their requests before using the single-slot directory and move mailboxes.
+
+## 2. Topology and submeshes
+
+The Server uses a `(1,1)` submesh. A Client either uses a separate `(1,1)` mesh or selects
+`client_coord` inside a larger model mesh. The roles may live in different processes and, for the
+static inter-mesh mode, on different hosts. Client construction verifies all of the following:
+
+- the selected Client coordinate is valid and differs from the endpoint node;
+- ordinary mode resolves both sides in the same Fabric mesh;
+- the chips are mutual direct neighbors under `FABRIC_1D`, or bidirectionally reachable through the
+  routing tables under `FABRIC_2D`;
+- both use the default `MetalContext` with fast dispatch; and
+- no other live Client owns the same `(client_mesh, client_coord)` pair.
+
+Under 1D every application route is one hop. Under 2D the request header carries an absolute route,
+and the sender opens the routing-table-selected first-hop direction and link. The Server enumerates
+reachable peers and builds a chip-id-to-connection lookup before serving requests. A parent with no
+peer is valid only for Server lifecycle tests.
+
+Static Galaxy-to-T3K routing is an explicit `FABRIC_2D` deployment mode because independent hosts
+may both assign mesh ID zero to their local control plane. A Galaxy endpoint Client routes request
+headers to the configured local gateway with the static-tunnel flag. The gateway Client opens the
+static endpoint directly; a non-gateway Client opens an ordinary local connection toward the
+gateway. A static T3K Server must run on the configured endpoint. Single-shard deployments open the
+configured static lanes directly; an eight-shard deployment groups worker cores `({0..7},0..2)`
+behind two lane-local Fabric MUXes in one persistent workload. Each Galaxy Client selects its Server
+worker group and lane independently, computes the gateway-to-client return route from the live
+Fabric graph coordinates, and carries that route in ESTABLISH. The Server validates the destination
+against the configured remote mesh, uses the route for its response, and retains it for that
+session. This avoids deriving a route from a fixed four-column mesh or assuming that the gateway is
+at `(0,0)`. The default constructors retain the ordinary 1D/2D behavior.
+
+Fabric-direct PUT and GET have an additional homogeneous-topology requirement. Each side computes
+remote DRAM addresses with its local bank map, so both chips must have identical DRAM channel and
+grid layouts. The PUT, batch-PUT, and GET resident programs check this when first built and fail
+rather than risking silent data corruption.
+
+The Server makes the first bottom-up L1 allocation on a fresh submesh allocator. The fixed bootstrap
+mailbox is an ABI offset from that allocator base, and the ESTABLISH response returns the actual base
+in `ServerDescriptor` for validation and subsequent operations. The Client owns an independent
+top-down L1 carve-out on its selected chip and sends its absolute base during ESTABLISH. Top-down
+placement avoids the model circular-buffer region when the Client shares the model mesh.
+
+The endpoint-free bootstrap supports Wormhole systems whose logical worker `(x,y)` maps to the
+canonical translated NoC coordinate `(18+x,18+y)`. Both roles validate the architecture and the
+selected Server worker-group coordinate before ESTABLISH. A system without NoC translation
+therefore fails closed instead of deriving a remote bootstrap coordinate from a
+harvesting-dependent local physical mapping.
+
+Do not allocate a physical chip's L1 through both its parent and submesh allocators while a cache
+role is alive. Those allocator instances do not coordinate.
+
+## 3. Wire protocol
+
+Deployment transfers no KV cache endpoint record. Standard intranode mode supplies the Server's
+`FabricNodeId`; static Galaxy-to-T3K mode obtains its route from the static Fabric configuration. The
+Client derives the fixed bootstrap mailbox from the matched TTNN/HAL ABI and writes one 80-byte
+`EstablishRequest` to the slot selected by its physical fabric chip ID. The request carries Client
+response addresses, operation L1 addresses, proposed value-ring depth, DRAM geometry, and a nonce.
+In static Galaxy-to-T3K mode, it also carries the versioned host-packed return route; in ordinary
+mode that word must be zero. The 96-byte `EstablishResponse` returns the Server-owned wire
+configuration and a generation-fenced session token. No registry ID, L1 locator, operation-ring
+configuration, or attach descriptor is assigned out of band.
+
+Both peers require the fused-EXISTS commit-prefix capability during ESTABLISH. This makes clients
+that omit a separate pre-EXISTS barrier fail closed with servers that do not implement the fused
+wait, and likewise rejects older clients whose requested contract does not include it.
+
+ESTABLISH retries with the same nonce and the same bootstrap and operation addresses until the
+configured bounded timeout. The Server reuses the token for an exact replay and does not rewrite
+direct-PUT session state. Bootstrap buffers remain allocated through clean DETACH, so a late replay
+response cannot target reused L1. DETACH leaves a nonce tombstone: later requests with that nonce,
+or a nonce different from an active session, are dropped without writing their untrusted response
+address. A different nonce may replace a retired tombstone. Exhausting the total timeout is outcome
+indeterminate and quarantines Client resources until reset.
+
+Every application packet starts with a 16-byte `PacketHeader`. The Fabric payload limit is 4,352
+bytes. Value packets add a 32-byte chunk header, leaving an aligned value unit
+`U = kMaxChunkValueBytes = 4,288` bytes. The Fabric limit is mirrored manually because the
+host/device-shared header cannot include the Fabric definition; keep it synchronized by hand.
+Compile-time assertions in `layout.hpp` pin the resulting internal alignment consequences.
+
+Active operation-ring request opcodes are PING, PUT_CHUNK, GET_CHUNK_REQ, EXISTS, REMOVE,
+MOVE_TO_HOST, MOVE_TO_DEVICE, DETACH, and COMMIT_BARRIER. ECHO, PUT_ACK, EXISTS_RESP,
+REMOVE_RESP, and DETACH_RESP are active replies or grant messages; some operations use completion
+atomics instead of a response payload.
+Bare PUT, GET, GET_RESP, and GET_CHUNK_RESP retain reserved numbers and have no active handler.
+
+### Direct PUT
+
+Only chunk 0 enters the Server request ring. It carries the key, total byte length, tensor metadata,
+and either a current adaptive lease or a request for an allocation grant. The current Server treats
+the tensor metadata as opaque and stores only the byte stream. ESTABLISH publishes the Client's
+immutable direct-PUT session state. The Server keeps at most one next-PUT lease.
+
+A PUT whose buddy order matches the lease can begin streaming while the Server selects the catalog
+slot. A size-class change or missing lease receives a PUT_ACK grant. Value chunks then Fabric-write
+the final interleaved Server DRAM pages; they are never staged in the Server packet ring. A separate
+flush-fenced completion atomic orders the Fabric channel before publication.
+
+The batch PUT operation uploads at most 128 descriptors and reuses one reader/writer workload and
+one Fabric session. Each item still follows the scalar wire protocol in input order. The batch
+operation is experimental and does not define a stable ABI or performance contract.
+
+### Direct GET
+
+GET sends one request containing the key, output DRAM base, page size, and packed capacity. On a
+hit, the Server writes chunks directly into Client DRAM and then sends a flush-fenced completion.
+The Server does not retain or return tensor metadata; the caller supplies the output tensor
+specification.
+
+Client GET always sends its request over Fabric and observes completion in Client L1. Server-host
+mapped ingress/completion is not a Client semantic path; every deployment uses the same
+GET_CHUNK_REQ ABI and output-durability contract.
+
+The blocking Client-L1 status read is the synchronous API boundary. The flush-fenced completion is
+sent only after output bytes are durable, and the command-queue read also drains the Client kernel's
+connection-close tail before the call returns.
+
+## 4. L1 and DRAM layout
+
+All L1 offsets are relative to the owning role's reserved base. Runtime sizing helpers in
+`layout.hpp` are the only source for carve-out sizes.
+
+The Server L1 working set includes:
+
+- termination, request-ring, and per-slot synchronization state;
+- the Client registry and direct-peer connection lookup;
+- PUT landed and host-commit counters;
+- the NCRISC/BRISC directory mailbox;
+- buddy allocator, exact residency-LRU, and metadata staging;
+- host-worker move, staging, and completion channels; and
+- GET circular-buffer and startup state.
+
+Optional GET ingress and admission workers reserve private L1 on their logical cores. Core `{0,0}`
+remains the only directory and Server-Fabric owner.
+
+The Client L1 working set includes response state, PUT staging and reader/writer rings, cumulative
+semaphores, the direct-PUT session configuration, and the adaptive lease descriptor. The
+experimental batch path allocates a descriptor page lazily.
+
+Server DRAM contains the catalog directory, non-authoritative hash index, buddy-managed value heap,
+buddy metadata, and the issue-ordered pending-host FIFO. A directory entry conceptually stores
+validity, key and value lengths, access epoch, device offset, host generation or pending token,
+location, buddy order, and key bytes.
+
+The hash index is an open-addressed acceleration structure maintained by the same serialized BRISC
+that owns directory updates. Every key-to-slot lookup, including same-key PUT replacement, re-reads
+the indexed directory entry and verifies validity and key bytes. Hash collisions or stale buckets
+therefore cannot override the directory, and catalog free-slot and LRU decisions remain independent
+of the index.
+
+The Server rejects configurations whose combined DRAM reservation exceeds 25% of device DRAM. Its
+runtime L1 checks cover the ring carve-out, key-count-dependent LRU links, and GET CB. Consequently,
+a value below an individual environment-variable hard cap can still fail construction in an
+oversized combination.
+
+DRAM NoC transfers require the L1 endpoint and DRAM address to have the same 32-byte phase. Header
+sizes, value-unit size, ring strides, staging addresses, and tensor page-size validation preserve
+this invariant. The `static_assert`s in `layout.hpp` must fail before a layout change can turn into a
+device hang.
+
+## 5. Write-through storage model
+
+A landed PUT first has a valid device copy and a `device-pending-host` directory entry. That copy is
+the only complete value until host write-through finishes, so it is pinned against replacement and
+eviction. After commit, every retained value has authoritative session-local host backing and may
+also retain a disposable device-cache copy.
+
+| State | Key retained | Host copy | Device copy |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| device pending host | yes | in progress | yes, pinned and authoritative for now |
+| device resident | yes | yes, authoritative | yes, disposable |
+| host only | yes | yes, authoritative | no |
+
+Device eviction changes metadata and frees a buddy block; it does not copy bytes. Removing or
+catalog-replacing an entry drops membership and its host backing.
+
+On an MMIO-capable Server with a usable NoC mapping, the host region contains a staging window,
+completion and ingress control, per-slot generation metadata, and an optional pinned value pool.
+Pool extents use first-fit allocation with coalescing. If a new value does not fit, existing pool
+residents remain in place and the new value is preserved in a host vector after using the staging
+window. Setting `KVM_SERVER_HOST_POOL_BYTES=0` selects this heap-vector representation intentionally.
+
+A 1 GiB hugepage is preferred because it supplies a large contiguous mapping and host pool. The
+4 KiB shared-memory fallback provides the control and staging region but normally no pool. When no
+usable NoC mapping is available, including on a non-MMIO Server, the worker performs targeted PCIe
+copies instead.
+
+Pool-resident values are read in place. Heap-vector values are copied into the staging window; a
+mapped host-tier GET publishes completed staging blocks only after ordering their bytes, allowing
+the BRISC to consume earlier blocks while the host fills later ones. The device addresses the
+mapping with a 32-bit offset, so construction rejects a usable region that would cross that offset
+boundary.
+
+Host-tier GET attempts read-through admission using the same allocator and device LRU as PUT. The
+outcome differs at the exceptional no-block boundary:
+
+- the mapped-host path can still stream the authoritative host value and leave it host-only;
+- the targeted-copy fallback needs a device block as its PCIe staging destination and reports a
+  miss when none can be allocated, even though the catalog entry remains retained.
+
+When an admission is accepted, Client completion waits until the device copy and directory state
+are durable.
+
+## 6. Operation semantics
+
+### ESTABLISH and PING
+
+ESTABLISH is the one-time bootstrap handshake. It publishes the Client node, core, L1 base,
+receive-buffer address, and local wire proposal. The Server creates the registry entry and returns
+the descriptor and token only after validating that immutable session state. Its bounded timeout
+protects the host from waiting forever; a possibly late response makes the session outcome
+indeterminate and quarantines Client resources. The corresponding
+Server registry entry remains unavailable until reset/restart.
+
+PING sends at most 1,024 payload bytes and returns the ECHO response. It is a lifecycle and route
+self-check, not a general data path.
+
+### PUT
+
+The Client reader pulls source-tensor bytes into an L1 value ring while the writer sends earlier
+entries over Fabric. On a lease hit, the stream can overlap Server catalog selection. On a fallback,
+the Client waits for the allocated final DRAM address. The writer drains each source batch before
+releasing ring entries, protecting both payload and header reuse.
+
+Before issuing its own prefetch reads, the reader clears per-transaction-ID outstanding state that
+may survive a preceding program on the same NIU. The reset must not run after this PUT has issued a
+read because it also discards valid in-flight accounting.
+
+After the final Fabric completion, the Server reads back the last device page as a visibility fence,
+then publishes the pending directory and hash state. The landed response also publishes the next
+lease when one was reserved.
+
+Host write-through has two scheduling paths:
+
+- PUTs of at least 2 MiB can stream complete prefix groups to a reserved host destination while
+  later Fabric groups arrive when NoC-mapped staging is available and the pending-host FIFO is
+  empty; and
+- other PUTs enter the issue-ordered pending-host FIFO and are committed by the host tiering worker
+  and BRISC.
+
+The streaming tail owns the move channel until it publishes host metadata. FIFO consumers patch the
+same host generation and final location in issue order. Both paths advance the cumulative commit
+counter only after the host bytes and directory update are durable.
+
+An asynchronous PUT returns at the landed/pending-publication boundary. The source tensor is
+reusable, but host write-through can remain in flight. NCRISC advances a device-owned issued counter
+before landed; BRISC advances a committed counter after host write-through. Committed PUT and FLUSH
+use COMMIT_BARRIER, which snapshots the issued prefix and returns a Fabric completion after the
+committed counter reaches it. GET, REMOVE, MOVE, and close use that explicit barrier where they
+require a committed prefix. EXISTS fuses the same wait into its Server handler before the directory
+lookup, avoiding an extra Fabric round trip without weakening ordering. The manager Server runtime
+uses those device-owned counters for teardown drains.
+
+### GET
+
+GET is synchronous from the caller's perspective:
+
+- **Device hit:** BRISC updates recency and fills the GET CB from the value heap. NCRISC streams the
+  CB directly into Client DRAM.
+- **Mapped-host hit:** BRISC or the host worker produces the same CB stream from a pool extent or
+  staging window. An optional admission worker mirrors CB batches into the allocated cache block.
+  Fabric consumption and admission have separate source-release handshakes.
+- **Fallback host hit:** the host worker first pushes the complete value into an allocated device
+  block, after which the ordinary device path serves it.
+
+When read-through allocation succeeds, completion waits for output durability and the admitted
+device copy. On mapped-path allocation failure, output can still succeed without residency. On
+fallback allocation failure, the lookup reports a miss as described in section 5.
+
+A miss leaves the output unchanged. An undersized output fails before changing output or access
+recency. A larger output is permitted only within the GET chunk limit derived from the configured
+maximum; bytes through the next 16-byte wire boundary are unspecified, and later bytes remain
+unchanged. Tensor dtype, layout, shape, and logical length are caller-known metadata.
+
+### EXISTS and DELETE
+
+Both operations synchronously use the BRISC directory mailbox. EXISTS does not update recency.
+DELETE removes the hash mapping and catalog entry, frees any buddy block, and sends the removed slot
+through the Server-internal move channel. The tiering worker reclaims host backing before the wire
+response is released.
+
+### MOVE
+
+Both calls wait until the Server directory path processes the lookup:
+
+- `move_to_host()` invalidates and frees the device-cache block before returning;
+- `move_to_device()` returns after best-effort admission is posted, before its host-to-device copy
+  and directory commit; and
+- a missing key or key already in the target state is an acknowledged no-op.
+
+The host-worker completion counter covers write-through, host serve, and explicit admission. It is
+an internal cumulative frontier, not a per-request MOVE token.
+
+## 7. Capacity, LRU, and pressure
+
+The value heap uses a buddy allocator. A raw byte length first rounds up to a 4,288-byte unit count;
+that unit count then rounds to the next power-of-two block. Buddy rounding is less than 2x relative
+to the already rounded unit count, but a very small value still consumes one full unit.
+
+BRISC maintains an exact doubly linked residency LRU. Device-resident and pending entries join it at
+publication. Pending entries remain pinned, so victim selection skips them and chooses the oldest
+fully committed resident. Eviction changes the entry to host-only, fences its header, frees the
+block, and coalesces it without a value copy.
+
+The adaptive PUT path may keep one same-buddy-order block in a lease state. It is not a directory
+resident or part of reported live bytes, but it consumes physical heap capacity. Under a steady
+size class, effective residency can therefore be one block below the raw heap size. The lease
+policy controls whether this speculative reservation may evict a committed LRU entry.
+
+Catalog retention is separate. New keys use never-used or recycled invalid slots. Only when both
+sources are exhausted does a new distinct key replace the catalog-wide LRU entry. That replacement
+forgets the key and drops its host backing regardless of device residency. Pending entries are not
+catalog victims.
+
+PUT publication, successful GET, and committed MOVE_TO_DEVICE admission update access recency.
+EXISTS does not. Capacity checks use buddy-rounded bytes, not raw value length.
+
+## 8. Lifetime, shutdown, and failure handling
+
+The manager owns lifecycle enforcement. Each process keeps its role's mesh alive. Deployment starts
+the Server role before the Client role, closes every Client role before the Server role, and releases
+meshes only after their role has closed. Quiesce a parent mesh before closing it.
+
+A Client close drains all issued PUT commits through COMMIT_BARRIER, then sends DETACH and waits for
+its response before releasing local resources, including the retained bootstrap buffers. A clean
+DETACH permits a later Client to establish
+with the same Server without clearing stored values. The Client owns no Server host object; registry
+entry, generation-fenced token, limits, L1 layout, and DRAM geometry arrive through Fabric
+ESTABLISH. The Server cannot observe host-process Client lifetime,
+so the manager control plane must ensure Clients are quiesced before Server shutdown. Client close
+supplies the remote durability boundary, and Server teardown drains the device-owned issued frontier
+after that coordination boundary.
+
+ESTABLISH uses a configurable bounded timeout. Client Fabric operations, including
+COMMIT_BARRIER, do not yet have a host watchdog; remote failure notification and bounded request
+completion remain manager lifecycle protocol work.
+
+Once a Client ESTABLISH or request may have reached a persistent worker, an exception can
+leave its outcome unknown. Client quarantines its own device-visible resources rather than freeing
+addresses a late operation might access; the Server remains available to other registry entries,
+while the abandoned entry requires reset/restart before reuse. An uncertain Server launch or
+teardown quarantines Server resources. Quarantines remain until process exit and device reset.
+
+Client connection and request resources have separate ownership. The manager lifecycle layer owns
+the bootstrap buffers and ESTABLISH workload; the TTNN operation runtime owns request L1,
+resident programs, and request keepalives. An indeterminate connection quarantines both sets, while
+an indeterminate established request first quarantines the operation runtime and then causes the
+manager lifecycle layer to retain its bootstrap ownership and registry slot.
+
+A manager Server termination failure is retryable: the runtime retains its workload, worker,
+buffers, and registry entry until termination is confirmed or the resources are quarantined.
+
+MOVE_TO_DEVICE completion is only a cache hint. Server teardown may abandon an outstanding
+admission without losing the authoritative host value; all session-local state is being destroyed
+anyway.
+
+## 9. Consistency and concurrency invariants
+
+1. Landed PUT completion follows device writes, the Server read-back fence, and pending
+   directory/hash publication. Commit completion additionally follows host write-through and the
+   final directory update.
+2. Every durable retained catalog entry has byte-identical host backing. A pending entry is
+   device-readable but is pinned until that backing exists.
+3. Device residency is a disposable cache property. Eviction does not change key membership;
+   catalog replacement and DELETE do.
+4. Sequential directory operations drain preceding PUT commits, providing read-after-write for the
+   supported calling pattern.
+5. GET never reports success before output writes are durable. An admitted read-through copy is
+   also durable before completion, but admission itself is best effort and fallback-dependent.
+6. Directory metadata, residency LRU links, and buddy owner/offset/order agree before an entry is
+   treated as device resident.
+7. Calls on one Client are serialized. The outer lifecycle mutex protects state and the opaque
+   handle through close/reset; the operation backend's inner mutex serializes request resources.
+   The lock order is always lifecycle then operation. Distinct Clients may submit independently,
+   and the Server device dispatch loop serializes shared directory state. Host-direct diagnostics
+   require externally quiesced Client traffic.
+8. Device sequences are cumulative `uint32_t` counters with wrap-aware progress comparisons. PUT
+   issued and committed frontiers share one device baseline and half-range ordering.
